    {
      "id": 80,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Reading these during a benchmark",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "prometheus"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 27,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 5
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "These are the Go runtime and process metrics the Prometheus client exposes for\nfree; nothing here is application-specific.\n\nDuring a load test (`./scripts/loadtest.ps1`), the two questions they answer are:\n**do goroutines come back down when the load stops**, and **does the consumer's\nmemory stay flat while a backlog builds**. The second is the claim the\nbackpressure design makes: the queue is bounded, so a backlog costs disk on the\nKafka broker rather than memory in the consumer.\n\nRecord what you see in `benchmarks/results/README.md` beside the throughput\nnumbers; a CPU or memory figure without the load that produced it cannot be\ncompared with anything."
      },
      "transparent": false
    }
  ]
